options.plain_explanation:
Today the target is a blob that wanders at random. Here it becomes prey: it has a small genome, and a population of prey evolves alongside the bodies. A prey is paid for escaping, within fixed physical limits:
- top speed;
- one stamina budget, used for both sprinting and dimming;
- how far away it can notice the dot, and how often it looks;
- how dim it can get.

Its fitness uses 'reduced virulence': it is highest when a prey escapes about 75% as well as the best prey. A prey that nobody can catch therefore earns less than one that only good bodies catch.

Every time the target is placed in a life, one prey is drawn from a mixture:
- 25% today's default target, which keeps the reflex rung paid;
- 50% the current prey population;
- 25% a hall of fame of past prey, which guards against cycling.

As bodies improve, prey get trickier in small steps, starting from zero difficulty. Generation 0 of this arm is exactly the base arm. Nobody names memory, prediction or discrimination. If dimming when approached, sprinting away or swerving helps prey escape, those pressures appear on their own, and they persist only if some bodies can partly answer them.
- Stage 0 needs no kernel change. The prey genome is the value of target keys that already exist (speed, turn rate, halo width, brightness), plus the scratch blink/dark keys and a decoy that looks like the target, costs exactly c_miss when clicked and so adds no new price.
- Stage 1 adds reactive prey: a tiny policy network, evaluated at the prey's own 'look' events, that can flee, swerve or hide when the dot comes near.

options.user_decision:
How it differs from the dropped c_miss ramp, plainly:
- The ramp was a lesson plan. We picked one key (a price), one direction (up) and the population thresholds, to teach one behaviour (do not click blind).
- Here no key is a price, mutations go in any direction, and a world stays only while measurements that name no capability say it is useful.
- The designer sets only the key ranges, the three thresholds, the archive size and the base share.
- What it shares with the ramp: which worlds bodies meet depends on how they are doing.

You must decide:
(a) whether undirected, measurement-gated dependence on the population is acceptable;
(b) the thresholds. Proposed: controller beats the best reflex by more than 3 SE on 256 fresh lives; at least 1 of 64 mutants beats its parent by more than 2 SE on re-score without fewer cells or clicks, used as a rank weight; best body trails the controller by 1 to 6 F;
(c) whether within-life keys (blink, flip, epochs; Q4) may enter;
(d) keeping 25% base lives.

options.what_changes:
KERNEL. Today the accountant owns one dot (X, Y, F, gam, P, O, strokes, key) and one heap per life. Multi-body needs:
- Ctx split into per-body state and a shared world (blobs, timers, turns);
- a merged scheduler that gives the accountant every body's time groups in one global order, with ties broken by body index;
- click arbitration;
- a placement rule for the shared target;
- optional rival brightness in catch_candidate;
- per-body ledgers and hash folds.
The same changes go into reference.py and lifesim.js.

ARENA. A torus or a tether, because on an unbounded screen bodies drift apart and stop interacting.

OUTER LOOP. Rival-per-slot pairing and a rival hall of fame.

CHEAPER STAND-IN. A rival forager inside the accountant: a parametric pursuer that can steal the target and coevolves. About 3-5 pd, but that is really option 1, not the population as environment.

recommendation:
Try coevolving prey first. It gets the most visible outcomes per CPU: about 1.05-1.2x today's cost, a curriculum that starts at zero difficulty, and prey behaviours you can watch. Run it in two steps, as a new arm C beside the plan's base arm B, with the same run seeds 100-111 so generation 0 of C equals B.

STEP 0 (laptop, scratch only, about a week, no repo edits):
(a) Gates using the user's sticks against six scripted prey settings, with the parallel agents' tools (openhr/harness and openhr/gradient/gdfe.py) and their own assigned seed blocks, about 5-6 CPU-h:
- fast-straight (v_t 0.25, tau_turn 200);
- dim (lam_s 1);
- small (sigma 0.4);
- blink 20/20;
- a mimic decoy priced at -c_miss;
- one combination.
Any setting where the information controller does not beat the best reflex is removed from the prey genome space before launch. Mutant improvement there is recorded as a rank weight.
(b) A pilot of per-slot prey on existing target keys plus the scratch blink/dark keys: arm C0 (coevolving, reduced virulence lambda 0.75, 25% base, 25% hall of fame) against arm D0 (the same prey space drawn at random), 3 seeds x 200 generations each. About 36 CPU-h, 5-6 h wall on 6-8 threads. Pass if all three hold:
- no disengagement: shortlist captures on current prey stay at least 30% of its captures on base targets, and F still varies across the population;
- prey move away from the default target;
- C0's best beats D0's best on held-out prey.

STEP 1 (8-11 pd): reactive prey in the kernel (look events with flee/swerve/hide, a softsign policy network, ABI 4 mirrored in reference.py and lifesim.js, null-prey bit-exact test). Then the matrix:
- C, coevolving prey;
- R, prey replayed from C's logs without feedback (Zaman's control);
- D, the prey space at random;
- B, shared with the plan, as the control.
Each arm is 12 seeds x 500 generations, about 180 CPU-h. The three new arms total about 540 CPU-h (range 350-850), about $10-25 on one 192-core spot box, within the plan's $120 cap if run after round 1.

Build the minimal-criterion world archive second, as arm A. It reuses the same three sticks for admission and should later hold prey genomes as well as world keys. Keep broad randomisation only as the no-feedback control (D). Run arm M as already planned. Defer multi-body competition until C shows niches forming, and do not use income sharing for selection.

Before Step 0 you need to decide:
1. Whether a world that responds to the population is acceptable when no designer chooses its direction and no price moves.
2. The prey's physical limits.
Seed blocks for the Step 0 gates must also be assigned; I had none.
